::ALG2_U02_L02_Q01::
Question ID: ALG2_U02_L02_Q01
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.PF.4
Question:
What is the vertex of f(x) \= (x + 1)² - 6?
{
=A. (-1, -6) #Correct. x + 1 means h \= -1.
~B. (1, -6) #Sign is reversed.
~C. (-6, -1) #Coordinates reversed.
~D. (1, 6) #Both signs are wrong.
}


::ALG2_U02_L02_Q02::
Question ID: ALG2_U02_L02_Q02
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.PF.4
Question:
What is the axis of symmetry for f(x) \= (x - 7)² + 2?
{
~A. x \= 2 #This uses the vertical shift k instead of the axis value.
=B. x \= 7 #Correct. x \= 7.
~C. y \= 7 #Axis is vertical.
~D. x \= -7 #Sign is reversed.
}


::ALG2_U02_L02_Q03::
Question ID: ALG2_U02_L02_Q03
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.PF.4
Question:
What is the minimum value of f(x) \= 3(x - 2)² - 5?
{
~A. 2 #This is x-coordinate.
~B. 3 #This is leading factor.
=C. -5 #Correct. Minimum output is -5.
~D. 5 #Sign is wrong.
}


::ALG2_U02_L02_Q04::
Question ID: ALG2_U02_L02_Q04
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.PF.4
Question:
What is the maximum value of f(x) \= -2(x + 4)² + 9?
{
~A. -4 #This is x-coordinate.
~B. -2 #This is leading factor.
~C. 4 #Sign is reversed.
=D. 9 #Correct. Maximum output is 9.
}


::ALG2_U02_L02_Q05::
Question ID: ALG2_U02_L02_Q05
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.PF.4
Question:
What is the y-intercept of f(x) \= x² - 3x - 10?
{
=A. -10 #Correct. f(0) \= -10.
~B. -3 #This is x coefficient.
~C. 10 #Sign is wrong.
~D. 1 #This is leading coefficient.
}


::ALG2_U02_L02_Q06::
Question ID: ALG2_U02_L02_Q06
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.PF.4
Question:
What are the x-intercepts of f(x) \= (x + 5)(x - 1)?
{
~A. (5, 0) and (-1, 0) #Signs are reversed.
=B. (-5, 0) and (1, 0) #Correct. Zeros are -5 and 1.
~C. (0, -5) and (0, 1) #These are not x-intercepts.
~D. (-5, 1) and (0, 0) #This mixes coordinates.
}


::ALG2_U02_L02_Q07::
Question ID: ALG2_U02_L02_Q07
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.PF.4
Question:
For f(x) \= (x - 3)² + 4, what is the range?
{
~A. y <\= 4 #This would open downward.
~B. x >\= 3 #Range uses y-values.
=C. y >\= 4 #Correct. Minimum output is 4.
~D. all real numbers #Output is restricted.
}


::ALG2_U02_L02_Q08::
Question ID: ALG2_U02_L02_Q08
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.PF.4
Question:
For f(x) \= -1(x + 2)² + 7, what is the range?
{
~A. y >\= 7 #It opens downward.
~B. x <\= -2 #Range uses output values.
~C. all real numbers #Output is restricted.
=D. y <\= 7 #Correct. Maximum output is 7.
}


::ALG2_U02_L02_Q09::
Question ID: ALG2_U02_L02_Q09
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.PF.4
Question:
A projectile model has zeros t \= 0 and t \= 6. What does t \= 6 usually represent?
{
=A. landing time #Correct. Later zero is landing.
~B. initial height #Initial time is usually t \= 0.
~C. maximum height #Maximum is at vertex.
~D. launch time #Launch is t \= 0.
}


::ALG2_U02_L02_Q10::
Question ID: ALG2_U02_L02_Q10
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.PF.4
Question:
What is the axis of symmetry for zeros x \= -2 and x \= 8?
{
~A. x \= 6 #Not the midpoint.
=B. x \= 3 #Correct. Average is 3.
~C. x \= -10 #This subtracts incorrectly.
~D. x \= 10 #This adds absolute values.
}


::ALG2_U02_L02_Q11::
Question ID: ALG2_U02_L02_Q11
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.PF.4
Question:
Which feature gives the highest or lowest point of a parabola?
{
~A. y-intercept #This is where x \= 0.
~B. x-intercept #This is where y \= 0.
=C. vertex #Correct. Vertex is turning point.
~D. domain #Domain is input set.
}


::ALG2_U02_L02_Q12::
Question ID: ALG2_U02_L02_Q12
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.PF.4
Question:
For h(t) \= -16t² + 64t, when is the object on the ground?
{
~A. t \= 2 only #This is maximum time.
~B. t \= 4 only #This is only landing.
~C. t \= 0 only #This is only launch.
=D. t \= 0 and t \= 4 #Correct. Factor as -16t(t - 4).
}


::ALG2_U02_L02_Q13::
Question ID: ALG2_U02_L02_Q13
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.PF.4
Question:
What is the vertex of f(x) \= x² - 8x + 12?
{
=A. (4, -4) #Correct. Complete the square.
~B. (-4, -4) #Sign is incorrect.
~C. (4, 12) #This is not vertex.
~D. (0, 12) #This is y-intercept.
}


::ALG2_U02_L02_Q14::
Question ID: ALG2_U02_L02_Q14
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.PF.4
Question:
Which interval describes where f(x) \= (x - 1)² decreases?
{
~A. x > 1 #It increases there.
=B. x < 1 #Correct. It decreases before vertex.
~C. x \= 1 #This is only vertex line.
~D. all x #It does not decrease everywhere.
}


::ALG2_U02_L02_Q15::
Question ID: ALG2_U02_L02_Q15
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.PF.4
Question:
Which interval describes where f(x) \= (x - 1)² increases?
{
~A. x < 1 #It decreases there.
~B. x \= 0 #This is one input.
=C. x > 1 #Correct. It increases after vertex.
~D. x <\= 1 #Includes decreasing side.
}


::ALG2_U02_L02_Q16::
Question ID: ALG2_U02_L02_Q16
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.PF.4
Question:
A ball reaches maximum height at t \= 3 seconds. What key feature gives this time?
{
~A. x-intercept #This gives ground time.
~B. y-intercept #This gives initial height.
~C. domain #Domain gives input interval.
=D. vertex #Correct. Vertex gives maximum.
}


::ALG2_U02_L02_Q17::
Question ID: ALG2_U02_L02_Q17
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.PF.4
Question:
What does the y-intercept represent in a height model h(t)?
{
=A. initial height #Correct. h(0) is starting height.
~B. landing time #Landing is an x-intercept.
~C. maximum height #Maximum is at vertex.
~D. axis of symmetry #Axis gives vertex time.
}


::ALG2_U02_L02_Q18::
Question ID: ALG2_U02_L02_Q18
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.PF.4
Question:
For f(x) \= 2(x + 3)² - 1, what is the axis of symmetry?
{
~A. x \= 3 #Sign is reversed.
=B. x \= -3 #Correct. Vertex x-coordinate is -3.
~C. y \= -1 #Axis is vertical.
~D. x \= -1 #This uses k incorrectly.
}


::ALG2_U02_L02_Q19::
Question ID: ALG2_U02_L02_Q19
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.PF.4
Question:
Which domain is usually meaningful for time in a real-world projectile problem?
{
~A. negative values only #Negative time is not meaningful.
~B. all real numbers always #Context restricts time.
=C. t >\= 0 within the flight interval #Correct. Time begins at launch.
~D. only t \= 0 #Motion includes more than launch.
}


::ALG2_U02_L02_Q20::
Question ID: ALG2_U02_L02_Q20
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.PF.4
Question:
What are the x-intercepts of f(x) \= x² + x - 12?
{
~A. (4, 0) and (-3, 0) #Signs are reversed.
~B. (0, -4) and (0, 3) #These are not x-intercepts.
~C. (-12, 0) and (1, 0) #These are not zeros.
=D. (-4, 0) and (3, 0) #Correct. Factors are (x + 4)(x - 3).
}

